One correct solution was to “fix” two problems:

1. When initializing the counter‐predicate register, the assembler does not accept writing to a register in the p8–p15 range using the “ptrue” mnemonic without a type suffix. (In the SVE “ptrue” instruction you must use a type suffix when writing to registers other than those used for “normal” predicates.) One acceptable fix is to write
  ptrue p8.b 
instead of
  ptrue p8
This tells the assembler that you want all “byte‐elements” (the minimal element size) active in p8. (When used later by the “cntw” instruction the type is ignored.)

