Below is one correct solution. In the original code the SVE instructions (dup, smulh, and add) require that the destination register be the same as the “first source” register. (For example, smulh is defined as

  SMULH Zd.<T>, Pg/M, Zd.<T>, Zm.<T>

so that the destination must be the same as the first source; similarly many SVE arithmetic instructions are “destructive” in that they write back into the first source.) Also, when duplicating a scalar into a vector of 32‐bit elements (i.e. .s) you must use the 32‐bit register (w1) not the full X register.

One acceptable fix is to generate your “true index” in z0.s; then copy it into z2.s so we can compute

  result = true index + (asr( smulh(true index, true index), #3 ))

in a destructive (in‑place) way. (Notice that the “add” instruction must write to the same register as its first source.) The complete fixed code follows.

<code>    .text
    .global main
    .type main, %function
main:
    // Standard frame setup
    stp     x29, x30, [sp, #-16]!
    mov     x29, sp

    // Compute allocation size for 128 integers (128*4 = 512 bytes)
    mov     x1, #128         // number of elements
    lsl     x1, x1, #2       // size in bytes (= 128*4)
    mov     x0, x1           // pass size in x0 to malloc
    bl      malloc           // pointer returned in x0

    // ---------- SVE Vectorized loop ------------
    // We want to compute for each element:
    //    result = index + asr( smulh(index, index), #3 )
    // where “smulh …” is the signed widening multiply that returns the high half,
    // and where (for many SVE instructions) the destination must equal the first source.
    //
    // x0 = base address of the allocated array
    // x1 = current element index (starting at 0)
    // x4 = total element count (128)
    mov     x1, #0           // loop index
    mov     x4, #128         // total element count

1:
    // Create predicate p0.s: active lanes where element index < x4.
    whilelo p0.s, x1, x4

    // Generate a vector of consecutive numbers in z0.s (will be 0,1,2,…).
    index   z0.s, #0, #1

    // Broadcast the current loop base (x1) into every lane.
    // (Since we want 32‐bit values in the vector, use w1!)
    dup     z3.s, w1

    // Compute the true index for this chunk: true index = (x1 + lane offset)
    add     z0.s, p0/m, z0.s, z3.s

    // Make a copy of the true index (in z0.s) into z2.s.
    // We need to compute smulh (which writes into its first source) without losing the true index.
    mov     z2.s, z0.s

    // Compute the high half of the signed multiplication of true index by itself.
    // Note that smulh requires the destination and the first source to be the same.
    smulh   z2.s, p0/m, z2.s, z0.s

    // Perform an arithmetic shift right by immediate 3 on the multiplication result.
    asr     z2.s, p0/m, z2.s, #3

    // Add the original true index (in z0.s) with the shifted product (in z2.s).
    // The destination (z0.s) must be the same as the first operand.
    add     z0.s, p0/m, z0.s, z2.s

    // Store the results (each element is 32‐bit) to memory.
    // The address is computed as x0 + (x1 << 2).
    st1w    { z0.s }, p0, [x0, x1, LSL #2]

